cartridge "APPLE WILLIE/Hubert Cross/1990"

' APPLE WILLIE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Apple Willie", COMPUTE!'s Gazette April 1990
' disk (Commodore 64 machine language: APPLE.ML at $7000, started by the
' APPLE.BOOT BASIC loader, which first draws 40 frames of scrolling
' scenery). No author is named in the program; it is credited to the
' magazine. Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026,
' in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (watched in jsc64
' and read from its machine code, orig/apple-ml.dis), the game is built for
' the ColecoVision: a walled garden at night in four parallax bands, Willie
' as a smooth two-colour sprite, swooping birds, a worm, red apples, sounds.
'
' The game: Willie walks left through the garden picking up apples. Birds
' swoop in from the left, high or low, and a worm crawls along the path;
' touching any of them costs a life. Duck under the high birds, jump over
' the low birds and the worm. Apples lie still: walk into them. 10 points an
' apple; three lives.
'
' From the original (orig/): Willie stays put and the world moves; walking
' left adds 3.5 C64 pixels a frame to everything (2.8 here) and scrolls the
' scenery; a new object every 40 + 0..40 frames into a free one of seven
' slots, of four kinds picked at random (1 in 4 each): high bird (2.375 C64
' pixels a frame), low bird (2.0), worm (1.5) and apple (still); down
' crouches (and stops walking), the button jumps (velocity -4, gravity
' 0.28 a frame; held down it jumps again on landing; no jump while
' crouching); in the air the stick is not read and a walk goes on; touching
' a creature costs a life after a pause, the round restarts without the
' objects and the score stays; an apple is worth 10.
'
' Liberties: the field is scaled (C64 X by 0.8, the jump and heights by
' 0.76: Willie is 16 x 32 here, 24 x 42 doubled there) and hits are box tests
' on the drawn bodies (the C64 used its sprite collision register and a
' 24 x 42 box); objects appear at column 2 and vanish before column 30
' (the C64 brought them in from behind the left border and out the right);
' the parallax is four bands repeating every 32 pixels (the C64 drew 40
' bitmap frames at the start); 4 walk frames (16); GAME OVER and the
' score, then the title (the C64 went back to PUSH JOYSTICK TO START).
' Standing still is safe from nothing (every creature walks into Willie)
' but scores nothing; as on the C64, seven apples waiting at the left fill
' every slot and stop new creatures until Willie walks.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): left walks, down ducks, either button
' jumps. On the title, up and down choose (START, HOW TO PLAY, CREDITS),
' a button chooses; HOW TO PLAY has two pages (a button turns the page,
' 30 s idle goes back).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

